/**
 * Creates follow-up tasks for opportunities coming out of the weekly
 * pipeline review.
 *
 * Tasks rejected by validation rules do not block the others; the method
 * returns the Ids of the opportunities whose task could not be created.
 *
 * Note: a single bulk Database.insert(records, false) is used instead of
 * per-record inserts. With 150-250 opportunities, individual inserts would
 * exceed the 150-DML-statement governor limit and fail the entire call.
 */
public with sharing class FollowUpTaskService {

    private static final Integer FOLLOW_UP_DAYS = 7;

    public static Set<Id> createFollowUps(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        Set<Id> failedOpportunityIds = new Set<Id>();

        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failedOpportunityIds;
        }

        List<Opportunity> opportunities = [
            SELECT Id, Name, OwnerId
            FROM Opportunity
            WHERE Id IN :opportunityIds
        ];

        if (opportunities.isEmpty()) {
            // No matching opportunities means nothing could be created.
            failedOpportunityIds.addAll(opportunityIds);
            return failedOpportunityIds;
        }

        Date dueDate = Date.today().addDays(FOLLOW_UP_DAYS);

        List<Task> tasks = new List<Task>();
        for (Opportunity opp : opportunities) {
            tasks.add(new Task(
                WhatId      = opp.Id,
                OwnerId     = opp.OwnerId,
                Subject     = 'Follow up: ' + opp.Name,
                ActivityDate = dueDate,
                Status      = 'Not Started',
                Priority    = 'Normal'
            ));
        }

        // allOrNone = false: records rejected by validation rules fail
        // individually without rolling back the successful inserts.
        Database.SaveResult[] results = Database.insert(tasks, false);

        for (Integer i = 0; i < results.size(); i++) {
            if (!results[i].isSuccess()) {
                failedOpportunityIds.add(tasks[i].WhatId);
            }
        }

        return failedOpportunityIds;
    }
}
```

I do one thing differently: the trigger does **not** make one future call per opportunity. Salesforce enforces a limit of **50 `@future` invocations per transaction**, so a CPQ batch of 200 opportunities would fail on record 51 with a `LimitException`, rolling back the whole batch. Instead the trigger deduplicates the affected `AccountId`s into a `Set` and makes **exactly one future call per transaction**, passing the whole set. The future method then recalculates all of those accounts in a bulk-safe way (one aggregate query, one DML). Recalculating per-account rather than globally also keeps the async job fast and within governor limits. Also note `Rating` is a picklist, so the values are set as strings matching the picklist values.

