/**
 * Matches imported leads to existing contacts by email address.
 *
 * Emails are compared case-insensitively and with leading/trailing whitespace
 * removed. Leads or contacts with a blank email never match. If several
 * contacts share an email, the first one in the provided list wins.
 *
 * The method is purely in-memory: it performs no SOQL and no DML, so it is
 * safe to call from within an import transaction.
 */
public with sharing class LeadContactMatcher {

    /**
     * For each lead, returns the Id of the first contact (in list order) with
     * the same normalized email, or null when there is no match.
     * The returned list has the same size and order as {@code leads}.
     */
    public static List<Id> matchByEmail(List<Lead> leads, List<Contact> contacts) {
        // Index contacts by normalized email; first occurrence wins, so only
        // insert when the key is not already present.
        Map<String, Id> contactIdByEmail = new Map<String, Id>();
        if (contacts != null) {
            for (Contact c : contacts) {
                String email = normalizeEmail(c.Email);
                if (email != null && !contactIdByEmail.containsKey(email)) {
                    contactIdByEmail.put(email, c.Id);
                }
            }
        }

        List<Id> result = new List<Id>();
        if (leads != null) {
            for (Lead l : leads) {
                String email = normalizeEmail(l.Email);
                result.add(email == null ? null : contactIdByEmail.get(email));
            }
        }
        return result;
    }

    /**
     * Trims and lower-cases an email. Returns null for null, empty or
     * whitespace-only input so blank emails never match.
     */
    private static String normalizeEmail(String email) {
        if (String.isBlank(email)) {
            return null;
        }
        return email.trim().toLowerCase();
    }
}
```

**Why I did it differently than the nested loop**

The nested loop is correct, but with the stated worst case (3,000 leads × 3,000 contacts) it performs up to 9,000,000 iterations inside a transaction that is already doing import work. Apex's per-transaction CPU limit (10 s) can be breached surprisingly quickly when the import job also runs validation rules, flows and triggers around this call — and a failure rolls back the whole import batch.

A `Map<String, Id>` lookup is the same amount of code, arguably no harder to read, and turns the work into two linear passes (O(leads + contacts)), so the matcher stays cheap no matter how the list sizes grow. Two subtleties are handled explicitly:

- **First contact wins:** the map only stores a contact if its email key isn't already present, so the first occurrence in `contacts` order is kept.
- **Blank emails never match:** `String.isBlank` covers null, empty and whitespace-only strings; those keys are never added to the map and never looked up.

If you ever did need the nested-loop semantics (e.g. comparing on something that can't be keyed), I'd still cap it or bail out early once a match is found — but here the map version is strictly better with no readability cost.

