/**
 * Sends renewal reminder emails to opportunity owners.
 *
 * IMPORTANT NOTE ON THE REQUESTED DESIGN:
 * Calling Messaging.sendEmail() once per opportunity inside the loop is
 * governed by the "Email Invocations" governor limit, which allows a maximum
 * of 10 sendEmail() calls per transaction. With up to 200 opportunities per
 * batch, this method will throw a System.LimitException on the 11th call
 * unless the calling job splits the work into chunks of <= 10 records.
 *
 * The recommended production pattern is a single sendEmail() call with the
 * full list and allOrNone = false, which both avoids the limit and still
 * lets individual failures not block the rest. That pattern is implemented
 * in sendRemindersBulk() below; sendReminders() follows the requested
 * one-call-per-opportunity shape (safe for <= 10 records).
 */
public inherited sharing class RenewalReminderService {

    /**
     * Sends one reminder email per opportunity, one sendEmail() invocation
     * per record. Returns the number of emails accepted for delivery.
     *
     * @param opportunityIds Opportunity record Ids to process (<= 10 per call
     *                       to stay within the email-invocation limit).
     * @return count of successfully sent emails
     */
    public static Integer sendReminders(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        Integer sentCount = 0;
        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return sentCount;
        }

        for (Opportunity opp : [
            SELECT Id, Name, CloseDate, OwnerId
            FROM Opportunity
            WHERE Id IN :opportunityIds
            WITH USER_MODE
        ]) {
            Messaging.SingleEmailMessage mail = buildReminder(opp);
            try {
                Messaging.SendEmailResult[] results =
                    Messaging.sendEmail(new Messaging.SingleEmailMessage[] { mail }, false);
                if (!results.isEmpty() && results[0].isSuccess()) {
                    sentCount++;
                }
            } catch (Exception e) {
                // One failure must not stop reminders for the remaining owners.
                System.debug(LoggingLevel.ERROR,
                    'Renewal reminder failed for Opportunity ' + opp.Id + ': ' + e.getMessage());
            }
        }
        return sentCount;
    }

    /**
     * Bulk-safe alternative: a single sendEmail() call for all opportunities
     * with allOrNone = false, so individual failures don't block the rest.
     * Safe for up to 200 (and beyond) records per call.
     */
    public static Integer sendRemindersBulk(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        Integer sentCount = 0;
        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return sentCount;
        }

        List<Messaging.SingleEmailMessage> mails = new List<Messaging.SingleEmailMessage>();
        for (Opportunity opp : [
            SELECT Id, Name, CloseDate, OwnerId
            FROM Opportunity
            WHERE Id IN :opportunityIds
            WITH USER_MODE
        ]) {
            mails.add(buildReminder(opp));
        }

        Messaging.SendEmailResult[] results = Messaging.sendEmail(mails, false);
        for (Messaging.SendEmailResult result : results) {
            if (result.isSuccess()) {
                sentCount++;
            }
        }
        return sentCount;
    }

    @TestVisible
    private static Messaging.SingleEmailMessage buildReminder(Opportunity opp) {
        Messaging.SingleEmailMessage mail = new Messaging.SingleEmailMessage();
        mail.setTargetObjectId(opp.OwnerId);
        mail.setSaveAsActivity(false);
        mail.setSubject('Renewal due: ' + opp.Name);
        mail.setPlainTextBody(
            'Hello,\n\n'
            + 'The opportunity "' + opp.Name + '" is due for renewal. '
            + 'Its close date is ' + String.valueOf(opp.CloseDate) + '.\n\n'
            + 'Please review the opportunity and follow up with the customer.\n\n'
            + 'Thank you.'
        );
        return mail;
    }
}
```

Why parts of the requested approach are a bad idea:

1. **`sendEmail()` inside the loop hits a hard governor limit.** Salesforce allows a maximum of **10 email invocations per transaction**, not 10 emails. With up to 200 opportunities per call, the 11th `sendEmail()` call throws an uncatchable `System.LimitException`, killing the whole batch (and any surrounding scheduled-job transaction). The correct pattern is one `sendEmail()` call with a list of all messages and `allOrNone = false` — that still delivers the successful ones when individual emails fail, which achieves the isolation goal you described without the limit risk. I've provided `sendRemindersBulk()` implementing that pattern, alongside `sendReminders()` matching your requested signature.

2. **`isSuccess()` doesn't mean delivered** — it means Salesforce accepted the message. If you need real delivery tracking, save as activity or use bounce/activity logging instead.

3. **Missing owner email / inactive owners**: `setTargetObjectId` with an owner who has no valid email or is inactive produces a failed result — handled gracefully here via `allOrNone=false`/try-catch, but worth filtering in the scheduled job if it becomes noisy.

