## Why I changed your approach

I kept your core goals — one query, no queries in loops, only eligible contacts touched — but changed **which records the query returns**, because loading *all* contacts will not work in production:

1. **Governor limits.** A single SOQL query can return at most **50,000 rows** (`Limits.getLimitQueryRows()`). Your org has ~2.3 million contacts, so `SELECT Id, AccountId, MailingCountry FROM Contact` would throw `System.LimitException: Too many query rows: 50001` on the very first account update. Heap would also blow up holding millions of Contact records in a map.

2. **You don't need the whole table.** The trigger fires on at most 200 accounts per batch, and only the subset whose `BillingCountry` changed can have affected contacts. Filtering `WHERE AccountId IN :changedAccountIds` uses the standard index on `Contact.AccountId`, so the query is selective and returns only the handful of relevant contacts (typically a few hundred rows at most, well within limits).

3. **Bulk-safety is preserved.** The query is executed exactly once per trigger invocation, outside any loop, and the DML is a single `update` of a list — the same pattern you asked for, just bounded to the data that can actually be affected.

Everything else follows your spec: contacts with a blank `MailingCountry` or one matching the account's *previous* `BillingCountry` get the new country; contacts with any other country (working abroad) and contacts of unchanged accounts are never queried or updated.

